---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Standards — SEEN IN KOREA"
  description="How SEEN IN KOREA verifies sources, handles corrections, distinguishes confirmed information from claims, and separates editorial reporting from commercial relationships."
>
  <main>
    <section class="listing-hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow">KOREA, VERIFIED.</div>
        <h1>EDITORIAL STANDARDS</h1>
        <p>
          How SEEN IN KOREA verifies information, reports claims and corrects the record.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="article-wrap article-body">

        <h2>Our standard</h2>
        <p>
          SEEN IN KOREA aims to make information about Korea useful, verifiable and clear.
          We distinguish confirmed facts from claims, marketing language, speculation and opinion.
        </p>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <p>
          We prioritize primary sources whenever possible, including government data,
          company announcements, official documents, direct interviews and first-hand reporting.
          When secondary reporting is used, we identify the original source whenever it can be verified.
        </p>

        <h2>Verification</h2>
        <p>
          Information is not treated as confirmed simply because it is widely repeated online.
          Material claims are checked against available evidence before publication.
        </p>

        <h2>Celebrity and product claims</h2>
        <p>
          We distinguish between official campaigns, confirmed product use and unverified claims.
          A celebrity appearing in a campaign does not automatically mean that person personally
          uses or recommends the product outside the campaign.
        </p>

        <h2>Interviews</h2>
        <p>
          Interview material is presented as the experience or opinion of the person interviewed.
          Quotes may be lightly edited for clarity without changing their meaning.
          We do not fabricate interviews, quotes or first-hand experiences.
        </p>

        <h2>Anonymous sources</h2>
        <p>
          Anonymous sourcing is used only when there is a legitimate reason to protect a source.
          When anonymity is granted, we provide readers with as much relevant context as possible
          without identifying the source.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections and updates</h2>
        <p>
          If a material factual error is identified, we correct it promptly.
          Stories may also be updated when new verified information becomes available.
          Significant corrections should be made transparent to readers.
        </p>

        <h2>Images</h2>
        <p>
          We prefer original, officially provided or properly licensed images.
          Illustrative or AI-generated images should not be presented in a way that could
          reasonably be mistaken for documentary evidence of a real event or product detail.
        </p>

        <h2>Artificial intelligence</h2>
        <p>
          AI tools may assist with research organization, drafting, translation or production workflows.
          AI-generated material is not automatically published. Editorial review and factual verification
          remain part of the publication process.
        </p>

        <h2>Editorial and commercial separation</h2>
        <p>
          Affiliate links, advertising, sponsorships and commercial partnerships may support SEEN IN KOREA.
          Commercial relationships do not determine editorial conclusions.
          Relevant commercial relationships are disclosed where appropriate.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections and editorial contact</h2>
        <p>
          To report a factual error, raise an editorial concern or contact the newsroom:
          <a href="mailto:seeninkorea@gmail.com">seeninkorea@gmail.com</a>
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
